The '''1990 United States Senate elections''' were held on November 6, 1990, with the 33 seats of [[Classes of United States senators|Class 2]] contested in regular elections. Special elections were also held to fill vacancies. The [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]] increased its majority with a net gain of one seat from the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]]. The election cycle took place in the middle of President [[George H. W. Bush]]'s term, and as with most other [[midterm election]]s, the party not holding the presidency gained seats in Congress. This was the first time since [[1980 United States Senate elections|1980]] where any party successfully defended all their own seats, and the first time where Democrats did so since [[1958 United States Senate elections|1958]]. It was also the last time where the Democrats (or any party) made net gains in three or more election cycles in a row, having last experienced net losses in the Senate in 1980.

These elections, along with [[2022 United States Senate elections|2022]], featured the smallest seat change in history since the passage of the [[Seventeenth Amendment to the United States Constitution|Seventeenth Amendment]] in 1913, with only one seat changing parties. This election featured Democrat [[Paul Wellstone]] defeating incumbent Republican [[Rudy Boschwitz]] in Minnesota. To date, this is the last cycle in which Democratic candidates won U.S. Senate elections in Oklahoma and Tennessee.

== Background ==
The election was held as part of the [[United States midterm election|midterm election cycle]] of [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] President [[George H. W. Bush]]'s term.<ref name=":10">{{Cite news |last1=Dewar |first1=Helen |last2=Yang |first2=John E. |date=November 6, 1990 |title=The Senate Incumbents Win Control Remains with Democrats |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/politics/1990/11/07/the-senate/88a317b4-abae-4a7c-9a3c-f65fe6b52b54/ |access-date=March 12, 2025 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]}}</ref> Historically, the President's party struggles during the midterms.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Elving |first=Ron |date=November 13, 2022 |title=The midterms didn't produce a wave. Here's what that's meant historically |url=https://www.npr.org/2022/11/13/1136103595/the-midterms-didnt-produce-a-wave-heres-what-thats-meant-historically |access-date=2025-04-01 |work=[[NPR]] |language=en}}</ref>

