{{Main|1990 United States Senate election in Delaware}}Incumbent Democratic [[Joe Biden]] won re-election to a fourth term, defeating Republican challenger Deputy [[Attorney General of Delaware]] [[M. Jane Brady]]. Brady decided to run because she felt that Biden's liberal voting record did not reflect the political positions of Delawareans.<ref name=":12">{{Cite AV media |url=https://www.c-span.org/program/interview/delaware-senate-campaign/151098 |title=Delaware Senate Campaign |date=August 19, 1990 |last=Brady |first=M. Jane |author-link=M. Jane Brady |type=Video |publisher=[[C-SPAN]] |time=00:36 |access-date=March 12, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250215163441/https://www.c-span.org/program/interview/delaware-senate-campaign/151098 |archive-date=February 15, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> The election had a turnout rate of under 40% of registered voters.<ref name=":03">{{Cite web |last=Leip |first=David |title=1990 Senatorial General Election Results - Delaware |url=https://uselectionatlas.org/RESULTS/state.php?year=1990&fips=10&f=0&off=3&elect=0&class=2 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150912111215/http://uselectionatlas.org/RESULTS/state.php?fips=10&year=1990&f=0&off=3&elect=0&class=2 |archive-date=September 12, 2015 |access-date=2025-03-11 |website=[[Dave Leip's Atlas of U.S. Presidential Elections]] |url-status=live }}</ref> Biden won in a landslide with over 60% of the vote improving on his 1984 margin, winning all three counties and all 41 [[Delaware House of Representatives|state house]] districts.{{Sfn|Harper|1991|p=12}}{{Sfn|Dennis|1985|p=2}}
