The election was fought between the Republican incumbent [[Jesse Helms]] and the Democratic nominee [[Mayor of Charlotte, North Carolina|Mayor of Charlotte]] [[Harvey Gantt]]. Helms won re-election to a fourth term by a slightly wider margin than the [[1984 United States Senate election in North Carolina|close election in 1984]].

Helms drew [[controversy]] for airing what became known as the "[[Hands (advertisement)|Hands]]" ad produced by [[Alex Castellanos]]. It showed a pair of white hands with the voiceover saying ''"You needed that job, and you were the best qualified. But they had to give it to a minority because of a racial quota."'' The ad prompted allegations of [[racism]].<ref>{{cite magazine|magazine=The Nation|url=https://www.thenation.com/article/apparently-real-racists-are-anti-racists/|title=Apparently, the 'Real Racists' Are... Anti-Racists?|first1=Jamelle|last1=Bouie|date=27 July 2012|access-date=20 December 2017|archive-date=December 22, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171222054528/https://www.thenation.com/article/apparently-real-racists-are-anti-racists/|url-status=dead}}</ref>
